<section id="home" class="panel active">
 <div class="heading"><div class="kicker">01 / Introduction</div><h2>Project overview</h2><p>Start here to understand the biological question and how each section of the model connects to the supplied presentation.</p></div>
 <div class="grid">
  <div class="card s4"><span class="chip green">Normal tissue</span><div class="stat">TCA + OXPHOS</div><p class="muted">With oxygen available, normal cells can oxidize glucose-derived acetyl-CoA through the TCA cycle and oxidative phosphorylation.</p></div>
  <div class="card s4"><span class="chip amber">Tumor tissue</span><div class="stat">Aerobic glycolysis</div><p class="muted">Cancer cells can convert a substantial portion of pyruvate into lactate even when oxygen is present.</p></div>
  <div class="card s4"><span class="chip purple">Main concept</span><div class="stat">Reprogramming</div><p class="muted">Metabolic pathways are altered to support cell growth, biosynthesis and adaptation to the tumor environment.</p></div>
  <div class="card s12"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><ol class="steps"><li>Trace glucose through glycolysis to pyruvate.</li><li>Explain how pyruvate enters mitochondria and supplies acetyl-CoA to the TCA cycle.</li><li>Describe how cancer cells can favor lactate production.</li><li>Explain the roles of HK-II, PKM2, LDH-A and PFK-1, as well as MYC, HIF-1α and RAS.</li><li>Connect metabolic reprogramming with proliferation, hypoxia, ROS and possible therapeutic research directions.</li></ol></div>
  <div class="card s12"><h3>Big-picture pathway map</h3><div class="pathway"><div class="node">Glucose<small>Cell nutrient</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Glycolysis<small>Cytoplasm</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Pyruvate<small>Branch point</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Two possible routes<small>Cell context matters</small></div></div>
   <div class="compare"><div class="card"><span class="chip green">Normal-cell route</span><p>Pyruvate → acetyl-CoA → TCA cycle → electron transport / oxidative phosphorylation.</p></div><div class="card tumor"><span class="chip amber">Cancer-associated route</span><p>Pyruvate → lactate, alongside changes in mitochondrial metabolism and biosynthetic pathways.</p></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card s12"><div class="note"><b>How to read the model:</b> The central comparison and the approximate ATP values follow the supplied PPT. Additional pathway detail is included to make the biology easier to explain during a demonstration. Metabolic behavior varies among cancer types and conditions; not every cancer cell completely switches off its TCA cycle.</div></div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="glycolysis" class="panel">
 <div class="heading"><div class="kicker">02 / First pathway stage</div><h2>Glycolysis: glucose to pyruvate</h2><p>Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm. It breaks one six-carbon glucose molecule into two three-carbon pyruvate molecules and captures some energy.</p></div>
 <div class="card"><h3>Pathway sequence</h3><div class="pathway"><div class="node">Glucose<small>6 carbons</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Glucose-6-P<small>Phosphorylated</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Fructose-1,6-BP<small>Activated sugar</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Two 3-carbon molecules<small>Split phase</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">2 Pyruvate<small>End product</small></div></div>
 <p class="tiny">Select each stage to read what happens. The enzyme names below are standard biochemical explanation added to expand the presentation's overview of glycolysis.</p></div>
 <div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px">
  <div class="card s5"><h3>Explore each stage</h3><div class="selector" id="glyButtons"></div></div>
  <div class="card s7"><span class="chip">Selected reaction</span><h3 id="glyTitle">1. Glucose phosphorylation</h3><div id="glyDetail" class="detail"></div></div>
  <div class="card s6"><h3>Energy investment phase</h3><p>Early glycolysis uses ATP to phosphorylate and prepare glucose for splitting. This investment helps retain and activate the sugar inside the cell.</p><ul><li>Two ATP are used in the investment phase.</li><li>The six-carbon sugar is rearranged and split.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="card s6"><h3>Energy payoff phase</h3><p>The two three-carbon intermediates are processed to form pyruvate. ATP and NADH are generated during these reactions.</p><ul><li>Four ATP are produced gross per glucose.</li><li>Net glycolytic gain is two ATP per glucose.</li><li>Two NADH are formed per glucose.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="card s12"><div class="note"><b>Why glycolysis matters in this project:</b> Both normal and cancer cells use glycolysis. The important difference discussed in the PPT is what happens to pyruvate afterward: mitochondrial acetyl-CoA/TCA metabolism or conversion to lactate.</div></div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="normal" class="panel">
 <div class="heading"><div class="kicker">03 / Normal-cell metabolism</div><h2>From pyruvate to the TCA cycle</h2><p>This section expands the normal-cell sequence shown in the PPT: glucose → pyruvate → acetyl-CoA → TCA cycle → oxidative phosphorylation.</p></div>
 <div class="card"><h3>Stage A · Pyruvate oxidation</h3><div class="pathway"><div class="node">Pyruvate<small>From glycolysis</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Mitochondrion<small>Entry into matrix</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Acetyl-CoA<small>Carbon enters cycle</small></div></div>
 <ol class="steps"><li>Pyruvate produced in the cytoplasm is transported into the mitochondrion.</li><li>The pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH) complex converts pyruvate into acetyl-CoA.</li><li>Carbon dioxide is released and NADH is generated during this conversion.</li><li>Acetyl-CoA combines with oxaloacetate to begin the TCA cycle.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="card" style="margin-top:14px"><h3>Stage B · TCA cycle reaction sequence</h3><p class="muted">The cycle takes place mainly in the mitochondrial matrix. Tap a reaction to view its role. This reaction-level detail is supplementary biochemistry to explain the TCA cycle named in the PPT.</p><div class="selector" id="tcaButtons"></div><div class="detail"><span class="chip">TCA reaction</span><h3 id="tcaTitle">1. Citrate formation</h3><div id="tcaDetail"></div></div></div>
 <div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px">
  <div class="card s6"><h3>Stage C · Electron transport chain</h3><p>Reduced carriers, mainly NADH and FADH₂, transfer high-energy electrons to protein complexes in the inner mitochondrial membrane. Electron transfer supports proton pumping across the membrane.</p><ol class="steps"><li>Electrons enter through respiratory-chain complexes.</li><li>Energy from electron transfer drives proton movement.</li><li>A proton gradient forms across the inner membrane.</li><li>Oxygen acts as the final electron acceptor and is reduced to water.</li></ol></div>
  <div class="card s6"><h3>Stage D · ATP synthase</h3><p>Protons flow back through ATP synthase. This movement powers the formation of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate. This process is called oxidative phosphorylation.</p><div class="note">The PPT uses approximately 36 ATP per glucose as its simplified normal-cell comparison value. Actual ATP yield depends on cellular conditions and accounting assumptions.</div></div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="cancer" class="panel">
 <div class="heading"><div class="kicker">04 / Tumor-cell metabolism</div><h2>How cancer cells redirect metabolism</h2><p>The PPT describes cancer cells as favoring aerobic glycolysis: glucose is converted to pyruvate and then lactate, even when oxygen is present.</p></div>
 <div class="card"><h3>Step-by-step cancer-associated route</h3><div class="pathway"><div class="node">Glucose<small>Uptake into cell</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Glycolysis<small>Increased pathway use</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Pyruvate<small>Branch point</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Lactate<small>LDH reaction</small></div></div>
 <ol class="steps"><li><b>Glucose uptake:</b> glucose enters the cell and supplies glycolysis.</li><li><b>Glycolytic processing:</b> enzymes convert glucose through a series of reactions to pyruvate.</li><li><b>Pyruvate branch:</b> instead of all pyruvate being oxidized through PDH, a portion can be converted to lactate.</li><li><b>Lactate formation:</b> lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) converts pyruvate to lactate while regenerating NAD⁺, which helps glycolysis continue.</li><li><b>Biomass support:</b> glycolytic intermediates can be diverted into pathways that supply building blocks for nucleotides, lipids and amino acids.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px">
  <div class="card s6"><span class="chip amber">Energy perspective</span><h3>Lower ATP per glucose</h3><div class="stat">~2 ATP</div><p class="muted">The PPT gives approximately 2 ATP per glucose from the glycolytic route, compared with approximately 36 ATP in its normal-cell comparison.</p></div>
  <div class="card s6"><span class="chip purple">Growth perspective</span><h3>Building-block supply</h3><p>Rapidly dividing cells need more than ATP. They also need carbon-containing precursors to make DNA/RNA, proteins and lipids. Glycolytic intermediates can feed biosynthetic routes.</p></div>
  <div class="card s12"><div class="note warning"><b>Important clarification:</b> Aerobic glycolysis does not mean that every mitochondrion stops working. Many cancer cells retain active mitochondria and use the TCA cycle for energy or biosynthesis. The PPT's phrase “partial mitochondrial suppression” is more accurate than saying mitochondria are completely inactive.</div></div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="warburg" class="panel">
 <div class="heading"><div class="kicker">05 / Central concept</div><h2>The Warburg effect explained</h2><p>The PPT attributes this observation to Otto Warburg: cancer cells can favor glycolysis and lactate production despite the presence of oxygen.</p></div>
 <div class="grid">
  <div class="card s7"><h3>What makes it unusual?</h3><p>In the simplified textbook expectation, oxygen availability supports mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation. The Warburg effect describes a cancer-associated pattern in which glycolysis and lactate production remain high even when oxygen is available.</p><ol class="steps"><li>Glucose is taken up and metabolized through glycolysis.</li><li>Pyruvate is produced in the cytoplasm.</li><li>A substantial amount of pyruvate is converted into lactate.</li><li>Glycolytic intermediates can be used for biosynthesis.</li><li>Lactate is released and can influence the surrounding tumor microenvironment.</li></ol></div>
  <div class="card s5"><h3>Explore the pathway balance</h3><p class="tiny">Conceptual illustration only — not measured data.</p><div class="barline"><div class="barlabel"><span>Normal-cell emphasis</span><span>TCA / OXPHOS</span></div><div class="barbg"><div class="barfill" style="width:88%"></div></div></div><div class="barline"><div class="barlabel"><span>Cancer-associated emphasis</span><span>Glycolysis / lactate</span></div><div class="barbg"><div class="barfill orange" style="width:88%"></div></div></div><p class="tiny">Bars show pathway emphasis as a teaching illustration, not a quantitative comparison.</p></div>
  <div class="card s6"><h3>Why it can support proliferation</h3><ul><li>Provides intermediates for nucleotide synthesis.</li><li>Provides carbon for lipid synthesis.</li><li>Provides precursors for amino-acid synthesis.</li><li>Can support adaptation to variable oxygen availability.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="card s6"><h3>Hypoxic tumor regions</h3><p>Some tumor regions have limited oxygen supply because blood-vessel delivery may not meet local demand. A glycolytic program can help cells maintain ATP production under low-oxygen conditions. The PPT notes this as one reason the pathway can support survival in hypoxic tumor cores.</p></div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="suppression" class="panel">
 <div class="heading"><div class="kicker">06 / Mitochondrial changes</div><h2>TCA cycle suppression in cancer cells</h2><p>The presentation identifies reduced pyruvate entry, reduced PDH activity, mutations in TCA enzymes and a proposed relationship with reactive oxygen species (ROS).</p></div>
 <div class="grid">
  <div class="card s6"><span class="chip amber">Step 1</span><h3>Reduced pyruvate entry</h3><p>Less pyruvate may be directed into mitochondrial oxidation. This can reduce the amount of pyruvate available for conversion into acetyl-CoA through PDH.</p></div>
  <div class="card s6"><span class="chip amber">Step 2</span><h3>Reduced PDH activity</h3><p>PDH links glycolysis to the TCA cycle by converting pyruvate into acetyl-CoA. Reduced PDH activity limits acetyl-CoA formation from glucose-derived pyruvate.</p></div>
  <div class="card s6"><span class="chip purple">Step 3</span><h3>TCA enzyme alterations</h3><p>The PPT names IDH, SDH and FH. Changes or mutations affecting these enzymes can disrupt normal TCA-cycle function and alter metabolite levels.</p></div>
  <div class="card s6"><span class="chip purple">Step 4</span><h3>ROS connection</h3><p>Reactive oxygen species are chemically reactive oxygen-containing molecules. Mitochondrial metabolism can contribute to ROS formation. The PPT proposes that suppression may reduce ROS that could otherwise damage DNA; the relationship is context-dependent.</p></div>
  <div class="card s12"><h3>Key TCA-associated enzymes mentioned</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Enzyme abbreviation</th><th>Full name</th><th>Role in the cycle</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>IDH</td><td>Isocitrate dehydrogenase</td><td>Converts isocitrate to α-ketoglutarate in an oxidative decarboxylation reaction.</td></tr><tr><td>SDH</td><td>Succinate dehydrogenase</td><td>Converts succinate to fumarate and is also part of respiratory-chain complex II.</td></tr><tr><td>FH</td><td>Fumarate hydratase</td><td>Converts fumarate to malate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <div class="card s12"><div class="note warning"><b>Interpretation:</b> The PPT gives a simplified overview. TCA-cycle behavior is not identical in every tumor; some cancer cells maintain substantial mitochondrial metabolism. Avoid describing TCA suppression as universal or complete.</div></div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="regulation" class="panel">
 <div class="heading"><div class="kicker">07 / Molecular regulation</div><h2>Key enzymes and oncogenes</h2><p>Explore the four enzymes and three regulatory factors specifically named in the presentation.</p></div>
 <div class="grid">
  <div class="card s6"><h3>Enzymes highlighted in the PPT</h3><div class="selector" id="enzymeButtons"></div><div class="detail"><h3 id="enzymeTitle">Hexokinase II (HK-II)</h3><div id="enzymeDetail"></div></div></div>
  <div class="card s6"><h3>Oncogenes and regulators</h3><div class="selector" id="geneButtons"></div><div class="detail"><h3 id="geneTitle">MYC</h3><div id="geneDetail"></div></div></div>
  <div class="card s12"><h3>How regulation connects to the pathway</h3><div class="pathway"><div class="node">MYC / RAS / HIF-1α<small>Regulatory signals</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Glycolytic enzyme expression / activity<small>HK-II, PFK-1, PKM2, LDH-A</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Increased glycolytic program<small>Glucose use and lactate production</small></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="node">Growth adaptation<small>Context-dependent</small></div></div><p class="tiny">This is a simplified teaching map of the relationships summarized in the supplied PPT, not a complete signaling network.</p></div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="comparison" class="panel">
 <div class="heading"><div class="kicker">08 / Side-by-side analysis</div><h2>Biochemical differences</h2><p>A consolidated comparison of the central points from the presentation.</p></div>
 <div class="card"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Normal-cell pattern in PPT</th><th>Cancer-associated pattern in PPT</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Main pathway emphasis</td><td>TCA cycle + oxidative phosphorylation</td><td>Aerobic glycolysis</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Pyruvate fate</td><td>Pyruvate → acetyl-CoA</td><td>Pyruvate → lactate</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Mitochondrial activity</td><td>Fully active mitochondria (simplified PPT description)</td><td>Partial mitochondrial suppression</td></tr>
 <tr><td>ATP per glucose</td><td>~36 ATP</td><td>~2 ATP through glycolysis</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Lactate</td><td>Low under aerobic conditions</td><td>Increased / constant production described</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Cellular needs</td><td>Energy production</td><td>Energy plus biosynthetic intermediates for proliferation</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Oxygen relationship</td><td>Oxygen supports oxidative phosphorylation</td><td>Glycolysis and lactate production can remain high despite oxygen</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px">
  <div class="card s6"><h3>ATP yield bars</h3><div class="barline"><div class="barlabel"><span>Normal cell</span><b>~36</b></div><div class="barbg"><div class="barfill" style="width:100%"></div></div></div><div class="barline"><div class="barlabel"><span>Tumor glycolysis</span><b>~2</b></div><div class="barbg"><div class="barfill orange" style="width:5.56%"></div></div></div><p class="tiny">Approximate figures exactly as presented in the PPT; actual yields vary.</p></div>
  <div class="card s6"><h3>What the comparison tells us</h3><p>The project is not simply about which pathway makes more ATP. It examines why a growing tumor cell may favor a pathway that yields less ATP per glucose but can supply metabolic intermediates and adapt to low-oxygen conditions.</p></div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="therapy" class="panel">
 <div class="heading"><div class="kicker">09 / Research significance</div><h2>Therapeutic implications</h2><p>The presentation lists possible research directions arising from metabolic differences between normal and cancer cells.</p></div>
 <div class="grid">
  <div class="card s6"><span class="chip">Direction 1</span><h3>Target glycolytic enzymes</h3><p>Research may investigate whether altered glycolytic enzyme activity can be used to slow cancer-cell growth. The PPT specifically highlights glycolytic enzymes as possible targets.</p></div>
  <div class="card s6"><span class="chip">Direction 2</span><h3>Investigate lactate production</h3><p>Because lactate can influence the tumor microenvironment, studying lactate production and transport may provide information about tumor biology.</p></div>
  <div class="card s6"><span class="chip">Direction 3</span><h3>Study mitochondrial function</h3><p>The PPT identifies restoration of mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation as a possible therapeutic concept. Whether this is useful depends on the specific cancer context.</p></div>
  <div class="card s6"><span class="chip">Direction 4</span><h3>Metabolic imaging and diagnosis</h3><p>Differences in glucose use and other metabolic features may be explored in imaging and diagnostic research.</p></div>
  <div class="card s12"><div class="note warning"><b>Educational note:</b> These are research implications from the presentation, not treatment recommendations. Cancer treatment decisions require qualified medical professionals and evidence from clinical studies.</div><h3 style="margin-top:17px">Conclusion</h3><p>Normal cells generally use mitochondrial oxidation efficiently when oxygen is available. Cancer cells can reprogram metabolism toward aerobic glycolysis, increase lactate production and use pathway intermediates to support proliferation. Enzymes, oncogenes, oxygen availability and mitochondrial changes all contribute to this complex metabolic behavior.</p></div>
